d side of any ad  on Facebook and you can tell us a lot of  things actually you can say if you never  want to receive an ad from that  advertiser again but you also can tell  us if you think this is a political ad  that should have been labeled how much  do you think people will engage with  this because sometimes and we've seen  certainly with social media people like  to reinforce their existing views of  things and then pass that along to other  people so how much do you think people  will actually look  to see who's behind the ad and really go  down that Lane we think people genuinely  want to understand who is trying to  influence them we also are going to make  an API available so news broadcasters  and third-party watchdogs can also look  out for whether or not someone's trying  to influence both political or issue ads  we remind people what an API is yes so  that allows people from a technology  standpoint so a third-party group to  access the information in a very  seamless way so they can watch to see  who is actually running those ads and  give us feedback so access the  information behind the ad to know who's  behind it so forth yes we think it's  incredibly important that people have  full transparency by the way not just  with political and issue ads we're  taking a broader view so that people can  see who are running ads across all  different types of categories not just  political and issue ads well Carolyn  this sounds all very good now but you  know Facebook has gone through some  dicey times Mark Zuckerberg was on  Capitol Hill did a major mayor copal  because people thought what took you  guys so long to make the changes I would  imagine you can't go through something  like this and not have a moment of  self-reflection within the company this  has been an incredibly important moment  for Facebook I've been at Facebook for  seven years and I think this is probably  the biggest cultural shift that I have  seen being led by mark but the entire  company understands the enormous  responsibility we have to protect  people's privacy to reduce the spread of  fake news and to ensure the integrity of  our elections and the entire company is  focused we're adding over 10,000 people  we're using technology to help us find  bad actors and bad behavior we have a  huge responsibility to over 2 billion  people and I think it's a real  understanding of that responsibility and  a commitment of resources you never  really control the privacy though that's  what I wonder can it can you ever really  do that one of the things that we are  very focused on is giving consumers full  control over their experience on  Facebook as a matter of fact in Europe  today there is a new data law that  rolled out called GDP our general data  protection regulation and what it  essentially is doing and we're rolling  it out here in the United States and  around the world is giving consumers  full control over their experience you  can tell us whether or not you want ads  to be  you can determine whether or not you  want us to use facial recognition and  your to Facebook is voluntarily putting  in place GDP our here in the United  States yes we are rolling out we're  calling it the global privacy experience  because GDP our isn't really relevant  for people here in the United States in  terms of terminology but yes the same  controls that are being made available  in Europe are rolling out globally we  want consumers to feel completely in  control of their Facebook experience if  they do not want targeted ads they do  not have to have them you have entertain  - if you don't want the targeted ads you  have to move remove your own political  preference on Facebook there are we are  giving people information around their  political affiliation their religion as  well as their relationship status and  they can remove that information if they  don't want that to be utilized and  targeted ads all right Carolyn Everson  thank you so much for coming in 